nt environments with a  consistent operational experience  that takes all of them into account  on prem we support vms running on vmware  vmware vsphere with bare metal support  coming soon  we interoperate with a large number of  storage and networking providers  so the platform can easily integrate  with your existing infrastructure  in the cloud we integrate with cloud  specific services  such as load balancers private virtual  clouds  virtual private clouds and identity and  access management  gcp and aws are currently fully  supported  with azure support coming soon  at the mobile edge we're partnering with  telecommunications companies  and providing a deployment option that's  optimized for the small footprint  large fleet size and the unique  networking requirements of that  environment  now on top of this distributed  infrastructure  ethos provides the tooling and the best  practices built in  for all different members of your team  for infrastructure operators we provide  a unified experience  for managing your kubernetes clusters  in your environments along with the  configuration policies required  to meet your enterprise security and  compliance needs  for service operators we provide a way  to gain visibility and control  of your services including traffic  management  authentication authorization monitoring  and alerting  and for developers we provide ways to  easily deploy  and run modern serverless workloads  without needing to learn about the  underlying infrastructure  all these components are built on open  source foundations  much of which was first developed at  google  with enthos we put the lego pieces  together  to give you a managed and supported  experience for your infrastructure  so that you can focus on your unique  business logic so let me show you in a  demo  a bit more about how all this works  we'll first look at some details of the  individual clusters  and then we'll take a look at the  unified management experience provided  on top  here i have created four enzo clusters  the first one is a gke cluster on aws  the second one is an eks cluster  also on aws  and the bottom two are gk clusters on  gcp  and those on aws just reached ga status  recently  and we've heard of a lot of customer  interest here so let's take a quick look  at how gke integrates with the aws  environment  under the hood the cluster is running as  a number of ec2 vms  in aws cluster control plane  consists of three vms that can be  deployed in a highly available fashion  as we have it here across three  availability zones  in the same region  the other three vms here belong to a  single  node pool deployed in a single  availability zone  you can also add in additional node  pools and put them in different  availability zones in that same region  note here that none of these nodes have  public ip addresses  for security reasons most of our  customers prefer  that the clusters are available only on  an internal network  and can only be accessed via a vpn or  similar mechanism  a lot of customers have well-established  aws vpcs  that have been through rigorous security  reviews  so gke is designed to be able to install  into the customer's own vpc  and stay within the established  boundaries  from a management perspective we're  starting with the foundation  that most customers are already familiar  with just like the kubernetes-based apps  that you deploy  and manage gke clusters and other and  those resources  can also be defined declaratively in a  yaml file  which can be stored in source control  and they can follow  similar processes like code reviews and  have manual approvals  before deployment additionally  you can enforce policies on it using the  same tools  that you use to enforce policies on apps  define enamel files  for example here is the yaml file i used  to define the gke cluster on aws that we  saw  we define here what region the cluster  should run in  what vpc to use what subnets to use  and so on with this  customers can then layer policies on top  such as to enforce  that only certain vpcs may be used for  gke clusters  now zooming out a bit we've just briefly  explored  how gke integrates with a native  environment  specifically in the case of running on  aws  you may be wondering how we provide a  unified operational experience  on top of such a large variety of  cluster deployment  that journey starts with a unified  authentication experience  many customers have standardized on a  few top identity providers  for their corporate authentication needs  such as microsoft active directory  opta and others  gke clusters support oidc openid connect  so that customers can authenticate using  the same familiar  corporate credentials to all of their  clusters across  all their deployment environments  instead of using gcp accounts for some  clusters  aws accounts for other clusters and so  on  let's see how this works  here i registered my gke on aws cluster  but i haven't yet authenticated to it  let's click the login button  in this case we're using octa as our  example corporate identity provider  so i'll enter in my octa credentials  here  and now i'm logged in  the process is similar on the command  line and also works the same way for  other and those clusters  after authentication i can now use this  web console  to see information about the cluster  i can see the nodes that make up the  cluster  and information about each node such as  the pods running on that node  now so far we've explored running gke on  aws  that's one option for running your  workloads you can also run gke  on other clouds or in your own data  center  you can attach your own existing eks or  aks cluster to enthos  and you'll soon be able to bring vms to  enthos as well  for example the second cluster i have  here in the web console  is an aws eks cluster that's been  registered  to my anthos environ an administrative  domain for anthos resources  once registered you can run the same  higher level and those services on top  such as configuration and policy  management as well as service management  with this option customers can keep  using their existing kubernetes  distribution  and can more easily onboard onto enthos  while relying on google to test and  support the integrated solution  aws eks and azure aks are supported  today  and more distributions will be supported  in the future  now if and when you'd like to move to  gke you'll get many additional benefits  you'll get a kubernetes distribution  backed by the company that created  kubernetes  and with the most experience in managing  it it'll be the same kubernetes  distribution everywhere with same  networking and identity stack  many of the same security features and  so on  you'll get a more consistent experience  for managing the cluster  and its life cycle  now switching gears we've just seen how  anthos provides a consistent management  experience  to access all my clusters across  deployment environments  beyond access many customers also need a  way to ensure  that their policies and configuration  can be applied across all clusters  reliably and uniformly and this is where  acm  and those configuration management comes  in  with acm you can have all of your  cluster configuration policies  defined in the central git repository  and that will be synchronized  and applied across all of your endless  clusters  this gives you a way to easily manage a  fleet of clusters  and prevent drift with continuous  monitoring  using git as the source of truth you can  manage your configuration  policies as code with the power of  source control  so you can integrate code review flows  be able to easily audit changes  and you can easily revert back to  previous versions  many of our customers use acm to apply  and enforce policies like  limiting host os access as well as  limiting container repo access  and shutting off external ingles  for this demo i've configured all four  of my clusters  to pull the configuration and policies  from the same git repo  they're all pulling from the same git  branch and they've all updated to the  latest commit  clicking into one of them  the git repo url is configured here  and the subdirectory we're using is  configured here  if i navigate to this git repo  and then the subdirectory  i can see the configuration and policies  that are being applied  specifically in this cluster directory i  have the cluster-wide configuration  policies  for example we have a policy here that  requires that all namespaces  have a cost center label for auditing  purposes  going back there's also a namespaces  directory  that defines a few namespaces that will  be managed by acm  for example in this file we create a  shipping dev namespace  with a cost center label  switching back to the terminal  we see that the shipping dev namespace  has been created by acm  let's see what happens if we try to  delete that namespace  we see that the namespace was deleted  and then immediately recreated by acm  about three seconds ago so acm  is constantly monitoring to make sure  that what's deployed  matches the source of truth in your git  reboot  now what if we were to try to do  something that violates our policies  we saw earlier that there's a policy  requiring that all namespaces have a  cost center label  if we try to create a namespace that  doesn't have such a label  this action is denied and the namespace  does not get created  with our cluster configuration and  policies in place  the next thing we might want to set up  is our ci cd pipeline  for this one simple option is to use  google cloud build  our fully managed serverless ci cd  platform  with google cloud build you can easily  define pipelines  consisting of arbitrary build steps run  as container images  they can be automatically triggered from  your source code repository  and run on google's infrastructure  for this demo i've set up a really  simple pipeline here  that performs three steps  first we do a git clone of the  kubernetes examples repo  then we update the service definition to  use a service of type load balancer  instead of node port  and finally we configure cube cuddle  and use it to deploy the guest book  sample app  now let's kick off the deployment  while it's running let's view the  progress in the web console  here we can see the three steps and the  output from each step  notice that we deployed this app to our  enthos gke on aws cluster  so while your cicd pipeline can be  running on google cloud  you can use it to deploy to any ethos  cluster including clusters on prem  and in other clouds  in this case our cluster is actually  sitting in a private vpc  in aws we're able to access it  because there's an agent running in that  cluster  that reaches outbound google cloud to  maintain a persistent connection  we didn't need to open up any firewalls  or expose any public ip addresses  for that cluster this ability to proxy  kubernetes control plane traffic  is currently in alpha  so this pipeline is pretty simple and it  finished quickly  you can of course include many more  steps such as steps that build container  images  run validation and unit tests and so on  all within this fully managed service  let's now move to the kubernetes page in  the web console  we can select the cluster to view  and we see here that our guestbook  workload has been deployed  in addition our guestbook service is  also running  and an aws elb elastic load balancer  has been allocated  we can click into that  view the guestbook which is currently  empty  and add an entry to it  with this cloud build integration you  can build your app once  using our serverless ci cd platform and  deploy it across multiple clouds  for customers that need to build jobs to  be able to access  private resources or need more  configurability  we're also offering a new feature called  custom worker pools  currently in limited beta where you can  run builds in your private network on  gcp  using vpc network peering  now after we've deployed our apps one  more thing we might want to do  is to be able to monitor them enthos  makes this easy with asm  and those service mesh which is our  managed version  of the open source seo project  with asm service operators can gain more  visibility and control  over your services including traffic  management  authentication authorization monitoring  and alerting  for this demo i deployed a sample online  boutique app  a few days ago on my two gke clusters on  gcp  with the asmui we can see at a glance  how all of our services are doing  we can also drop into a topology view to  see how the services communicate with  each other  from here we can drill into each service  to get more information about that  service  we can see any slos we set for the  service  as well as a lot of metrics for the  service  such as requests per second  error rate latency  request size and so on  as of the latest release gke clusters on  gcp  can be part of the same service mesh so  they can load balance between them  and we can aggregate the metrics for the  services running on them  so the metrics we see here are  aggregated from both of our gke clusters  that are on gcp  if we want we can also break this down  by cluster  to monitor each cluster separately  for example let's do that for this  latency graph here  with this view we can see how the  services in each cluster  perform relative to each other the red  line represents one cluster  and the blue line represents the other  taken together these monitoring and  multi-cluster capabilities  make asm a great tool for operating your  services  note though that these capabilities are  currently only available  for gke clusters on gcp and will be  coming soon to end those clusters  running on prem  and on other clouds  to wrap up we just saw in the demo today  how we can bring workloads running on  other clouds to anthos  whether you choose to use gke or another  kubernetes distribution  we saw a unified authentication and  management experience  from the web console we applied the same  configuration policies  to all of our clusters we deployed an  app to a gke cluster on it  on aws using google cloud build  and we saw how we can use antho service  mesh to monitor the services  running in our clusters there's a lot  more we didn't have a chance to cover  today  so check out the other sessions this  week as well as our website  thecloud.google.com  thanks for watching and stay safe

